{"ld": 1967, "paper": "HP1311", "session": "132", "title": "Resolve, to Design a Maine Home Energy Navigator and Coaching Pilot Program (TITLE CHANGE)", "sponsor_id": "h-kessler-christopher", "cosponsor_ids": [], "committee_code": "EUT", "committee_name": "Energy, Utilities and Technology", "summary": {"text": "This resolve directs the Maine Office of Community Affairs to establish the Maine Home Energy Navigator and Coaching Pilot Program to support energy efficiency of residential properties, enhance consumer protection and expand access to energy assistance programs, particularly for low-income and underserved communities. The pilot program will perform in-person and remote home energy assessments to identify energy-saving opportunities and assess current home performance and, if applicable, provide an independent, 3rd-party analysis of contractor recommendations; educate homeowners about the benefits of energy efficiency improvements; help low-income homeowners and homeowners in underserved communities understand and navigate their options to affordably meet their home energy needs; and engage in community outreach efforts to improve the accessibility and inclusivity of energy efficiency programs in this State. The pilot program must commence no later than October 1, 2025.\n\nThe resolve requires the Maine Office of Community Affairs to submit by June 30, 2027 to the joint standing committee of the Legislature having jurisdiction over energy and utilities matters a report including a summary of the pilot program's outcomes and any recommendations for continuing, expanding or modifying the pilot program.\n\nThe Maine Office of Community Affairs is required to use existing funds to support the pilot program and may seek additional funding from various sources to support the pilot program's implementation. Financial support may be provided to nonprofit organizations selected to participate in the pilot program for the direct costs of energy coaching, and up to 20% of the financial support may be used for indirect costs associated with energy coaching. The Maine Office of Community Affairs may also provide in-kind support, such as technical assistance and educational materials, to nonprofit organizations selected to participate in the pilot program.", "official": true, "source": "Bill text, https://legislature.maine.gov/legis/bills/getPDF.asp?paper=HP1311&item=1&snum=132"}, "status": "enacted", "status_label": "Finally Passed", "final_disposition": {"label": "Finally Passed", "date": "2025-06-18", "governor_action": "Signed", "governor_action_date": "2025-06-18"}, "fiscal_status": "Not Yet Determined", "documents": [{"label": "Bill text (LD 1967)", "url": "https://legislature.maine.gov/legis/bills/getPDF.asp?paper=HP1311&item=1&snum=132", "item": 1}, {"label": "Amendment C-A (H-618)", "url": "https://legislature.maine.gov/legis/bills/getPDF.asp?paper=HP1311&item=2&snum=132", "item": 2}], "amendments": [{"id": "C-A (H-618)", "description": null, "sponsor": null, "status": "Adopted by House & Senate", "url": "https://legislature.maine.gov/legis/bills/getPDF.asp?paper=HP1311&item=2&snum=132", "fiscal_note_url": "https://legislature.maine.gov/legis/bills/bills_132nd/fiscalpdfs/FN196702.pdf"}], "committee_actions": [{"date": "2025-05-21", "action": "Work Session Held", "result": null}, {"date": "2025-05-21", "action": "Voted", "result": "Divided Report"}, {"date": "2025-06-10", "action": "Reported Out", "result": "OTP-AM/ONTP"}], "committee_reports": [{"label": "Majority report", "recommendation": "Ought To Pass As Amended", "signer_count": 8, "signers": ["Senator Mark Lawrence (Chair)", "Representative Melanie Sachs (Chair)", "Representative Valli Geiger", "Senator Nicole Grohoski", "Representative Christopher Kessler", "Representative Walter Runte", "Representative Sophia Warren", "Representative Kilton Webb"], "signer_ids": ["s-lawrence-mark", "h-sachs-melanie", "h-geiger-valli", "s-grohoski-nicole", "h-kessler-christopher", "h-